+# NAUGHTY BEAR: PANIC IN PARADISE, NPUB30828 and NPEB01054 -- ours. The game draws through one raw
+# SPU that interprets a command stream the PPU builds each frame, and in play that SPU is the frame
+# rate's limit (99-100% busy on an Odin 3, 11-04). A profile of it put about a quarter of its time in
+# two copies its compiler wrote as one byte per pass (lqx, cbx, rotqby, shufb, stqx each byte):
+# - LS 0x9e90 (18%): a handler copying a command's payload out of the stream chunk to a destination
+#   that 0x9e58 has just aligned to 16 bytes. Now 16 bytes a pass: two quadword loads around the
+#   source, rotated and merged with selb, one aligned store; the last bytes, and any unaligned
+#   destination, still go through the original loop.
+# - LS 0x12b8 (9%): 64 bytes from the stack (sp+0x20, always aligned) to r43. Now four quadwords
+#   when r43 is aligned, the original loop otherwise.
+# Both were run against the original loops in an SPU interpreter on 5,000 random cases (lengths
+# 0..600, any source alignment, aligned and unaligned destinations): identical memory every time.
+# The code lives in the main loop's bookmark padding (16 nops after each SPU_Set_Bkmk_Tag write, a
+# channel RPCS3 ignores), which the first bookmark now steps over. Offsets are LS addresses.
+# Ps3PatchRepo.BUNDLED must list these, or they are imported but never enabled.
